//! member: true
//!
//! std.x.crypto.p256 — the NIST P-256 curve (secp256r1) and ECDSA
//! signatures: FIPS 186-5 (ECDSA), SEC1 v2 (point encodings and
//! validation), NIST SP 800-186 (the domain parameters), RFC 6979
//! (deterministic nonces). Stability tier: x — the D53 crypto ladder's
//! Weierstrass rung (campaign stdc-crypto, sprint sc23), the curve
//! real-world certificate authorities issue: sc22's `unsupported_alg`
//! answer for an ECDSA-P256 certificate becomes a real verification.
//!
//! **The nursery banner (D31).** `std.x.crypto.p256` is not
//! `std.crypto`: its path, names and behaviour may change or vanish in
//! one sprint; no other std module may depend on it. Its reason and
//! graduation trigger live in `std/x/README.md`. ECDSA's SHA-256 and
//! RFC 6979's HMAC are `std.x.crypto.sha2`'s, called through the
//! module boundary — a resident-on-resident edge the graduation move
//! must resolve (named in the register beside curve25519's).
//!
//! ## The design ruling: table-free constant-time scalar multiplication
//!
//! P-256 is a short Weierstrass curve, and the FAST implementations use
//! secret-indexed table lookups — a cache-timing surface no vector set
//! can see. The human-approved ruling (the sc23 contract): this module
//! multiplies with a TABLE-FREE ladder — 256 fixed iterations of one
//! complete doubling, one complete addition and one ARITHMETIC point
//! selection (`fe_select`: the secret bit enters only as a multiplier,
//! never a branch, never an index) — over the COMPLETE addition
//! formulas of Renes–Costello–Batina (eprint 2015/1060, the a = -3
//! specialization), which are total: identity, doubling and inverse
//! inputs take the same code path as a generic addition, so no
//! exceptional-case branch exists for a timing channel to read.
//! `scalar_mul` is c28's VERIFICATION TARGET for this module (marked in
//! its header): slower than a windowed table, and provably
//! constant-time by construction in portable wolf — c28's ct tier then
//! VERIFIES the property instead of compensating for a table. The
//! masked-table and hardware-intrinsic fast paths are named residue
//! (their own D-question when a measured performance need appears).
//!
//! ## The field: 16 checked-`int` limbs of 16 bits, Solinas reduction
//!
//! p = 2^256 - 2^224 + 2^192 + 2^96 - 1 (SP 800-186 §3.2.1.3, a
//! Solinas prime). The fast reduction is the identity
//! 2^256 ≡ 2^224 - 2^192 - 2^96 + 1 (mod p): product limb k (k >= 16)
//! re-enters at k-16 (+), k-10 (-), k-4 (-), k-2 (+). Sixteen bits is
//! the ONE uniform limb width that fits wolf's int story both ways
//! (the sc18 bound-proof discipline, stated before the field was
//! written): a 16x16 schoolbook column is at most 16 products < 2^32,
//! so a column is < 2^36 (measured 2^35.4 over the fuzz corpus), and
//! 96/192/224/256 are all multiples of 16, so the fold is limb-aligned
//! (32-bit limbs overflow a column at 2^68; 24-bit limbs misalign
//! 2^224). The carry tail adds the constant 2^32·p — which dominates
//! any signed fold output, so no intermediate is ever negative-beyond-
//! bound — then runs four carry passes folding the 2^256 overflow back
//! through the identity; the largest value anywhere is the limb-15
//! constant addition at 2^48.0 (measured), 32768x under int_max, and
//! X3's checked arithmetic enforces the whole proof at every operation:
//! a violated bound is a trap the vectors catch, never a silent wrap.
//! NO `wrapping[T]` anywhere; no bitwise-on-int (F-0026's spelling).
//! Every algorithm here was fuzzed limb-for-limb against a big-integer
//! reference before this file was written (3000 random + adversarial
//! field cases, 60 point-formula rounds incl. the complete-formula edge
//! cases, the RFC 6979 vectors end-to-end).
//!
//! ## The scalar field mod n: Montgomery, because n is not Solinas
//!
//! n (the group order) has an irregular low half, so mod-n arithmetic
//! is Montgomery multiplication in base 2^16 (CIOS): fixed schedule,
//! no division, no data-dependent trip counts. Inversions mod n and
//! mod p are Fermat powers to FIXED PUBLIC exponents (n-2, p-2) — the
//! per-bit branch reads a compiled-in constant, never data.
//!
//! ## ECDSA: verify is public, sign is the secret path
//!
//! VERIFY (FIPS 186-5 §6.4.2) handles only public data — a public key,
//! a message, a signature — so it carries no constant-time obligation
//! (the sc18 rule, stated per fn). The on-curve check of SEC1 §3.2.2
//! is MANDATORY on every ingested point: an attacker-supplied point
//! off the curve is the invalid-curve attack class, rejected by name
//! (`invalid_curve`, `check_point`) and answered `false` by `verify`.
//! SIGN (§6.4.1) is the secret path: the nonce and the scalar
//! multiplication are its obligations, enumerated for c28 in the fn
//! headers. **Nonces are RFC 6979 deterministic** — derived from the
//! key and message through HMAC-SHA-256, drawing NO entropy. The
//! ruling's lean, taken, and justified: nonce REUSE (or bias) leaks
//! the private key outright, deterministic derivation removes the
//! hazard class entirely, it needs no os capability (this module stays
//! pure and comptime-refusable only through its sha2 delegate), and it
//! is byte-for-byte testable (RFC 6979 A.2.5 pins k, r and s). s118's
//! `os_random` is the sanctioned entropy source when a consumer needs
//! randomized signatures; that variant is named residue, not built.
//!
//! ## ct: obligations, enumerated (c28's checklist for this module)
//!
//! - `scalar_mul` — THE target: fixed 256 iterations, complete
//!   formulas, arithmetic selection; the secret bit is a multiplier.
//! - `fe_select` — the selection itself (sc18's `fe_cswap` class).
//! - the RFC 6979 HMAC chain — fixed-schedule digest work (sha2's).
//! - recorded exceptions, all shared with the ladder's precedent: the
//!   0..255 ingestion guard branches per input byte (F-0035's class);
//!   `floordiv`'s sign correction (sc18's note); the 6979
//!   candidate-acceptance compare (`bytes_geq_n`) — the RFC's own
//!   rejection sampling, at most one extra HMAC round per 2^32 keys.
//! - VERIFY and everything it calls: public data, no obligation.
//!
//! Byte sequences are `List[int]` with the 0..255 element contract
//! (F-0035); all multi-byte values are BIG-endian (SEC1/FIPS
//! convention — unlike curve25519's little-endian RFCs). `to_hex` is
//! this module's own rendering (the ladder precedent).

use std.x.crypto.sha2

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------
// The field element: sixteen 16-bit limbs, little-endian by limb
// (limb i holds bits [16i, 16i+16)). Fields are interim backing, never
// API — a forged Fe produces a wrong result, never a trap. Carried
// limbs are < 2^16; transiently a limb may hold a full schoolbook
// column (< 2^36) between construction and the next fe_carry.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------

struct Fe {
    l0: int,
    l1: int,
    l2: int,
    l3: int,
    l4: int,
    l5: int,
    l6: int,
    l7: int,
    l8: int,
    l9: int,
    l10: int,
    l11: int,
    l12: int,
    l13: int,
    l14: int,
    l15: int,
}

